is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF adjacency on the R1–CSW1 transit link (CD_WAN_LINK_1), specifically R1 eth0 (10.0.0.33/30) to CSW1 (10.0.0.34). Mechanism: When the OSPF neighbor to CSW1 dropped and then re‑formed, internal routes toward the WAN via that core path reconverged. Existing TCP flows continued (kept-state and ECMP hashing), but new flows occasionally hit the reconverging path, causing SYN timeouts before succeeding once OSPF stabilized—seen by users as “slower and flaky” Internet connections to 203.0.113.x.

Localization: On R1, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows two neighbors: 10.0.0.77 (CSW1) on eth0 and 10.0.0.78 (CSW2) on eth1. The CSW1 neighbor 10.0.0.77 has very recent uptime (≈45s) versus ≈51s for CSW2, indicating a fresh adjacency reset on the CSW1 side. Direct evidence on the implicated device: CSW1’s FRR log records an OSPF adjacency re‑establishment with R1 at 09:40:07 (“Packet[DD]: Neighbor 10.0.0.76 Negotiation done (Master).”), confirming the drop/re‑formation on CSW1’s eth0 toward R1.

Root cause: An OSPF neighbor flap between R1 and CSW1 on the 10.0.0.32/30 transit (likely a brief link/daemon interruption on CSW1).

Corrective fix: Stabilize the R1–CSW1 transit by checking CSW1’s eth0 physical link and errors, verify FRR/OSPF process health and timers, and review any recent interface or bonding changes on the core. Enable detailed OSPF and interface logging to capture future events; consider BFD on the transit to speed convergence while monitoring for physical or process-induced flaps.
=== END ===